; 01-prop.kurt

; So far the only formula we could write was `true`.  To make claims about
; anything else, Kurt first needs to know which symbols stand for
; propositions, i.e. things that can be true or false.  That's what `prop`
; declares:

prop A
prop B, C     ; a comma-separated list declares several symbols at once

; Declaring a symbol a statement does not make it true, or tell Kurt anything
; about when it holds -- it only says "this is the kind of thing that can be
; proved", so that later formulas built from it, e.g. `A implies B`, make
; sense to the type checker.

; `true` still holds, of course:
true

; Try uncommenting the next line: Kurt will complain that it cannot derive
; `A`, because we declared A a statement but never said anything else
; about it.  Lesson 02 ("use") shows how to fix that.
;
;     A
